WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.900
LiteLLM.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:06.551
What happens when your model provider goes down in the middle of a demo?

3
00:00:06.551 --> 00:00:08.667
LiteLLM makes that a routing event.

4
00:00:09.867 --> 00:00:15.633
LiteLLM gives you one OpenAI shaped interface over more than a hundred providers.

5
00:00:15.933 --> 00:00:20.867
OpenAI, Anthropic, Bedrock, Vertex, even your own vLLM server.

6
00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:24.267
Switching models becomes a string change, not a rewrite.

7
00:00:25.467 --> 00:00:30.733
On top, it adds features every multi provider system rebuilds badly by hand.

8
00:00:31.033 --> 00:00:34.433
Fallback routing: if the primary fails, try the next model.

9
00:00:34.733 --> 00:00:37.600
Load balancing across deployments of one model.

10
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:40.300
Budgets per key, team, or project.

11
00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:43.133
And rate limits, with retries and cooldowns.

12
00:00:44.333 --> 00:00:45.433
Here is the chain.

13
00:00:45.733 --> 00:00:47.100
Your app makes one call.

14
00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.033
It goes to the primary first.

15
00:00:49.333 --> 00:00:52.533
If that fails, LiteLLM tries the next provider.

16
00:00:52.833 --> 00:00:54.400
And then your own local server.

17
00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.467
LiteLLM runs in two ways.

18
00:00:57.767 --> 00:01:02.000
As a library inside your Python process, or as a proxy gateway.

19
00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:07.867
With the proxy, keys, budgets, and routing live in one config instead of in every codebase.

20
00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:13.167
That is the standard answer for resilience and cost control without touching every app.

21
00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:15.900
Here is the call in Python.

22
00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.667
The primary model is just a provider and model name string.

23
00:01:19.967 --> 00:01:23.967
The fallbacks list names Claude next, then your own vLLM model.

24
00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:30.067
Add two retries and a twenty second timeout, and the response has the same shape from any provider.

25
00:01:31.267 --> 00:01:32.567
Picture a demo day.

26
00:01:32.867 --> 00:01:35.667
Your primary provider is down for forty minutes.

27
00:01:35.967 --> 00:01:39.884
The agent does not notice: LiteLLM fails over to Claude.

28
00:01:39.884 --> 00:01:41.633
Later a budget cap trips.

29
00:01:41.933 --> 00:01:46.167
Then local vLLM takes over, and your traces show the model flipping.

30
00:01:47.367 --> 00:01:48.196
Let's recap.

31
00:01:48.196 --> 00:01:52.067
One OpenAI shaped call reaches over a hundred providers.

32
00:01:52.367 --> 00:01:55.833
You get fallbacks, load balancing, budgets, and rate limits.

33
00:01:56.133 --> 00:01:59.033
Run it as a library, or as a proxy gateway.

34
00:01:59.333 --> 00:02:02.960
And failover becomes configuration, not an incident.

35
00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:03.867
Next up: Ray.
